{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And against all the gods of Egypt I will execute judgments</b> — those of wood rotted, and those of metal melted. The verse does not elaborate on these judgments, because after all the reproof of vanities is wood. So too, at the time of the event, it is written (Exodus 12:29), \"And HaShem struck every firstborn of man and every firstborn of beast,\" and it does not mention judgments upon their gods, for they were terror-stricken by the death of the firstborn, as it says (v. 30), \"And Pharaoh rose at night, he and all his servants and all of Egypt,\" and the judgments upon their gods were not known until morning, when they went to the houses of their abominations. And it is written, \"And the Egyptians buried those whom HaShem had struck among them, every firstborn, and upon their gods HaShem executed judgments\" (Numbers 33:4).<br>Now in my opinion, the verse alludes to the celestial princes, \"the gods of Egypt,\" in the manner of \"HaShem will punish the host of the height in the height, and the kings of the earth upon the earth\" (Isaiah 24:21) — He abased their mazal and the princes of the mazalot that are over them. And the verse alludes and is brief regarding what is hidden:" "<b>I will execute judgments, I am YHWH.</b> I Myself and not by means of the agent. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 12:12). But the midrash of the Sages is not so; rather: <b>And I will pass through the land of Egypt</b> — I and not an angel; <b>and I will strike every firstborn</b> — I and not a seraph; <b>and against all the gods of Egypt I will execute judgments</b> — I and not the agent; <b>I am YHWH</b> — I am He and no other. Its meaning is that because this passage is the speech of Moses with Israel, it would have been fitting to say, \"And HaShem will pass through the land of Egypt and strike every firstborn,\" and therefore they expound: <b>And I will pass through</b> — I Myself, not by means of an angel sent from Him for plagues, as in the case of David (II Samuel 24:16) and Sennacherib (II Kings 19:35). <b>And I will strike every firstborn</b> — I and not a seraph, to say that the very blow will be by the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He, not like a king who takes vengeance on his enemies before him by means of his speculatores — who are the seraphim from whom consuming fire goes out against his foes, as in the case of Elijah with the captains of fifty. <b>And against all the gods of Egypt I will execute judgments</b> — and not by means of the agent sent from Him, blessed be He, for all the deeds done on earth, who is the great angel who is therefore called Metatron. For the meaning of the word indicates a guide (pointer of the way), as it says in Sifrei (Ha'azinu, Shalach), \"The finger of the Holy One, blessed be He, became Metatron for Moses and showed him all the land of Israel.\" And in Tanchuma (cited in Aruch, entry מטטר): \"And Balak heard that Balaam had come\" (Numbers 22:36) — that He sent Metatron before him. And further there: \"See, I have begun to deliver before you\" (Deuteronomy 2:31) — \"I do not care for you; I am your Metatron.\" And do not wonder, for I am destined to act as a metator before an uncircumcised man, before Cyrus, as it says, \"I will go before you\" (Isaiah 45:2); before a woman I am destined to go first, before Deborah and Barak, as it says, \"Does not HaShem go out before you?\" (Judges 4:14), and so in many places. I have also heard that \"agent\" in Greek is \"metatar.\" And they expounded: <b>I am YHWH</b> — I am He and no other — He is One and there is no elohim with Him to stay His hand. This is the substance of this midrash."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:06:29.113307467Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}